(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-4-cms-exam-4-master-exam-form-a"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 4 Master Exam — Form A","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"An 81-year-old woman with no history of atherosclerotic disease asks whether she should start a statin for prevention. Her low-density lipoprotein is 145 mg/dL. What is the most appropriate approach?","choices":["Automatic high-intensity statin","Refuse any statin due to age","Clinical assessment and risk discussion","Calculate the Pooled Cohort risk"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Above 75 in primary prevention the approach is clinical assessment and a risk discussion rather than an automatic prescription. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"A 27-year-old man has clusters of small yellowish-white papules (eruptive xanthomas) on his elbows after a weekend of heavy drinking. His triglyceride is 900 mg/dL and his low-density lipoprotein is normal. Which lipoproteins are most likely extremely elevated?","choices":["Low-density lipoprotein and lipoprotein(a)","Chylomicrons or very-low-density lipoprotein","Intermediate-density lipoprotein alone","High-density lipoprotein and apolipoprotein A-I"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Eruptive xanthomas are usually seen with extremely high chylomicrons or very-low-density lipoprotein, and alcohol inhibits hepatic fatty-acid oxidation, raising triglyceride. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which cause of aortic regurgitation is a disease of the aortic root rather than the valve?","choices":["Marfan syndrome","Rheumatic fever","A bicuspid aortic valve","Infective endocarditis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Marfan syndrome is an aortic root disease, alongside aortic dissection, cystic medial degeneration, familial aneurysm, aortitis and hypertension. The root dilates and pulls the cusps apart. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 40-year-old man is newly found to have heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia with low-density lipoprotein of 250 mg/dL. He has read about a new injectable and wants it instead of any pill. Which option is inappropriate as initial therapy?","choices":["Evolocumab before trying a statin","A high-intensity statin","A low saturated-fat, low-cholesterol diet","Addressing smoking and blood pressure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The steps must be followed: high-intensity statins first, then ezetimibe and nicotinic acid, and the injectable only for patients refractory to drug therapy. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"What is a normal left ventricular ejection fraction?","choices":["50% or higher","80% or higher","70% or higher","30% or higher"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Normal ejection fraction is 50% or more, and it is the dividing line for treatment: 50% or higher is preserved ejection fraction, 49% or less falls into a reduced group. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man arrives with blood pressure 232/128, confusion and blurred vision. Over the first hour, how far should his mean arterial pressure be reduced?","choices":["By at least half, to remove the immediate danger","By no more than about twenty to twenty-five percent","No reduction until imaging is complete","To below 130/80 within the first hour"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, targeting around 160/100 to 110 over two to six hours and gradual normalization over a day or two. Autoregulation has adapted to the higher pressure, so dropping it to a chronic goal acutely can cause ischemic stroke, myocardial injury or renal failure. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with an occluded coronary artery is being rushed to the catheterization laboratory. His wife asks how quickly heart muscle is permanently damaged. Which time frame is most accurate?","choices":["About 40 to 60 minutes","About 24 to 48 hours","About 5 to 10 minutes","About 6 to 8 hours"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The heart is only 0.3% of body weight but consumes 7% of total oxygen demand, and irreversible injury occurs in about 40 to 60 minutes of ischemia. That is why rapid reperfusion matters. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman has a holosystolic murmur, and the examiner is unsure whether it is tricuspid or mitral in origin. Which bedside maneuver best separates the two?","choices":["Standing up quickly","Sustained handgrip","Deep inspiration","Full expiration"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Inspiration increases venous return to the right heart, so the tricuspid regurgitation murmur grows louder (the Carvallo sign) while mitral regurgitation does not. That distinguishes them. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 71, 9"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man has palpitations and fatigue. There is a holosystolic blowing murmur at the apex that radiates to the axilla and is loudest in the left lateral decubitus position (lying on the left side). Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Mitral valve prolapse","Mitral regurgitation","Tricuspid regurgitation","Aortic stenosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Blowing means regurgitation and holosystolic narrows it to the mitral or tricuspid valve; the apex, the radiation to the axilla and the left lateral position mark mitral regurgitation. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 48, 73"},{"q":"What is the treatment of choice in homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia?","choices":["Fibrate monotherapy with diet","Lifestyle and dietary change alone","Low-density lipoprotein apheresis","Moderate-intensity statin alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Apheresis, like dialysis, is the elective removal of low-density particles from the circulation; most patients also need more than one drug, starting with high-intensity statins. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes and hypertension has no cardiac symptoms. Her echocardiogram is normal. Which drug class is recommended first to lower her risk of heart failure?","choices":["Mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist","Beta blocker as the first agent","Loop diuretic such as bumetanide","Sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Risk factors with a normal heart and no symptoms are stage A, at risk for heart failure, and the one strong drug recommendation there is a sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor in patients with diabetes, not a beta blocker. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman with an hour of chest pressure at rest has ST depression in two consecutive leads and a standard troponin I of 0.3 ng/mL. Which diagnosis fits?","choices":["Stable angina","An ST-elevation myocardial infarction","Unstable angina","A non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. ST depression greater than 1 mm and/or T-wave inversion in two or more consecutive leads with an elevated troponin is a non-ST-elevation infarction. A standard troponin I above 0.04 ng/mL is abnormal, and 0.3 ng/mL is well above it. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 47; Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026 - SUPPLEMENT slide 72a"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with prior heart attacks collapsed without any prodrome while climbing stairs and woke within 20 seconds. Which referral should accompany her cardiac testing?","choices":["Physical therapy referral","Cardiology consultation","Neurology consultation","Dietitian referral"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Arrhythmic, structural and vascular cardiogenic syncope each pair their tests with cardiology consultation. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man remains above goal despite the described sequence: adherence confirmed, diuretic optimized, three agents titrated, spironolactone added and tolerated. What now?","choices":["Refer to a hypertension specialist","Stop spironolactone and restart from a single agent","Add a second mineralocorticoid antagonist","Accept the current pressure as his individual goal"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Referral is reserved for truly difficult cases after the full sequence has been worked through rather than being an early substitute for it. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman has recurrent anginal chest pain during periods of work stress and anxiety. Her electrocardiogram shows ST changes during symptoms, and coronary angiography shows normal coronary arteries. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Unstable angina","Stable angina","Microvascular angina","Vasospastic angina"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Microvascular angina (formerly cardiac syndrome X) is angina with ST changes, possibly with elevated biomarkers, but normal coronary arteries on the angiogram; it is more frequent in women and often associated with stress or anxiety. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 93, 94"},{"q":"Which description defines syncope?","choices":["Dizziness and tunnel vision without collapse","Prolonged unconsciousness needing support","Collapse with confusion lasting an hour","Brief loss of consciousness, then full recovery"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Syncope is a transient, self-limited loss of consciousness with inability to maintain postural tone, followed by spontaneous recovery. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction is comfortable at rest but becomes breathless after only a few steps across a room. Which New York Heart Association class describes him?","choices":["Class III","Class IV","Class II","Class I"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Symptoms with only slight exertion, such as a few steps across a room, and none at rest are class III. Class II is symptoms with ordinary exertion and class IV is symptoms at rest. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man had 30 minutes of chest pressure at rest. His electrocardiogram shows nonspecific T-wave changes, and his first troponin I is not abnormal. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Discharge him home now","Order a calcium score","Give a thrombolytic agent","Repeat the troponin I"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Troponin is drawn serially: when one is drawn, a second follows, because the change over time is what matters. A rising troponin I would turn this presentation into an infarction. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 32, 47; Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026 - SUPPLEMENT slide 72a"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman with neurogenic orthostatic hypotension asks whether she can keep a vasopressor at home for bad days. What should she be told?","choices":["A home supply is reasonable","Pressors are hospital-only drugs","Only if she avoids salt","Only after a tilt-table test"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Epinephrine, norepinephrine, dopamine, phenylephrine and vasopressin are listed for the emergency department and intensive care only. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 7-year-old boy with homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia and under 2% of normal receptor activity asks, through his parents, why he needs frequent sessions attached to a machine. Which explanation is most appropriate?","choices":["It filters the bad cholesterol out","It lowers his triglyceride level","It replaces his missing liver cells","It cures the disorder in weeks"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Apheresis is like dialysis: the elective removal of low-density particles from the circulation, and it is the treatment of choice for the homozygous disorder. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has progressive leg edema and jugular venous distension (distended neck veins). At the apex there is a low-pitched diastolic rumble with an opening snap. Which is the most likely cause of her right-sided heart failure?","choices":["Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Mitral valve stenosis","Tricuspid regurgitation","Aortic valve stenosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A rumbling diastolic murmur at the apex with an opening snap is mitral stenosis, one of the three causes of right-sided failure with chronic left-sided failure and pulmonary arterial hypertension. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"An 81-year-old woman has progressive breathlessness and ankle edema over three months. Her N-terminal pro-B-type natriuretic peptide is 720. What does this level suggest for her age?","choices":["Heart failure is likely","Heart failure is ruled out","Only a younger cut-off applies","The level is normal for her age"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Older than 75, an N-terminal fragment above 450 makes heart failure likely. In a younger patient the threshold would be 125. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 68-year-old smoker with peripheral arterial disease has blood pressure of 168/94 despite three agents. An abdominal bruit is audible, ultrasound shows a right kidney 2 cm smaller than the left, and he has had two episodes of sudden pulmonary edema. What is the most likely underlying cause?","choices":["Renovascular disease","Chronic kidney disease from diabetes","Primary aldosteronism","Obstructive sleep apnea"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The cluster is characteristic: resistant pressure, an abdominal bruit, asymmetric kidneys and recurrent flash pulmonary edema. In an older patient with established vascular disease the mechanism is atherosclerotic stenosis, often bilateral and progressive. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with reduced ejection fraction is started on spironolactone alongside her loop diuretic. Which counseling point is most accurate?","choices":["It replaces the need for her beta blocker","It is only a short course for congestion","It wastes potassium like her loop diuretic","It spares potassium, so levels are checked"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Spironolactone is a mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist, a potassium-sparing diuretic, which is why her potassium and renal function will be followed alongside the potassium-wasting loop. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 3-year-old boy has his pressure recorded at a well visit, and a new staff member flags it as low by comparing it with an adult's normal range. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Measure it again in adult units","Start a vasopressor for hypotension","Order an echocardiogram at once","Compare it with norms for his age"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Blood pressure norms are affected by age, so a child's reading is judged against the normal range for his age rather than an adult's. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 25-year-old woman fainted once after standing for an hour in the heat at a graduation, with warmth and nausea first and rapid recovery. Her examination and readings are normal. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Begin midodrine daily","Refer for echocardiography","Start fludrocortisone","Advise avoiding the trigger"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Vasovagal syncope is treated by trigger avoidance, here prolonged standing in the heat, with safety precautions when unavoidable. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman had her diuretic dose increased 1 week ago for high readings. She now feels lightheaded whenever she stands and nearly fainted at church. Supine she is 132/78 with a heart rate of 70; standing she is 108/68 with a heart rate of 96. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Vasovagal reflex syncope","Non-neurogenic orthostatic hypotension","Chronic cardiogenic hypotension","Neurogenic orthostatic hypotension"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Medication and volume loss are typical non-neurogenic causes, and the heart rate rise from 70 to 96 shows the autonomic reflex is intact. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which apolipoprotein is critical in triglyceride clearance?","choices":["Apolipoprotein(a)","Apolipoprotein A-I","Apolipoprotein B-48","Apolipoprotein E"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Apolipoprotein E is important in triglyceride metabolism and critical in triglyceride clearance, which is why its mutation lets chylomicron and very-low-density remnants accumulate. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What imaging is required when dissection is suspected, and what does a normal chest radiograph mean?","choices":["Immediate cross-sectional vascular imaging","Imaging can wait until blood pressure is controlled","A chest radiograph alone is sufficient to exclude it","Echocardiography is the definitive study"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The radiograph may show a widened mediastinum, but it cannot rule the diagnosis out, so a normal film must not be allowed to end the workup. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man remains at 158/96 on three agents at maximally tolerated doses. What is the commonest explanation to exclude first?","choices":["Progressive renal artery stenosis","An incorrectly sized cuff","Medication nonadherence","Undiagnosed primary aldosteronism"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it heads a list that also includes measurement error, white coat effect, high sodium or alcohol intake, interfering drugs, and undiagnosed sleep apnea or secondary hypertension. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man's pressure is 100/64, which is normal for many adults but far below his usual 150/90, and he now feels lightheaded. How should this reading be interpreted?","choices":["As normal, since it is above 90","As proof of autonomic failure","As relative hypotension for him","As a measurement artifact"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hypotension is a fall below normal, and normal is judged against the patient's age, comorbidities and own baseline, so a relative fall can be significant. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which drug combinations are preferred as initial therapy?","choices":["An angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor with an angiotensin receptor blocker","A beta blocker with a non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker","Two thiazide-type diuretics together","A renin-angiotensin blocker with a calcium blocker or diuretic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, because the classes act by complementary mechanisms. A single-pill combination is preferred where two agents are started, since it improves adherence. That means an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor or angiotensin receptor blocker paired with a dihydropyridine or a thiazide-type diuretic. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 54-year-old man arrives at the emergency department with 30 minutes of chest pressure. Which pair of studies does every patient with chest pain receive?","choices":["Treadmill stress test and D-dimer","Coronary angiography and echocardiogram","Electrocardiogram and chest radiograph","Calcium score and a lipid panel"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. All chest pain patients get a chest radiograph and a stat electrocardiogram. Blood work (complete blood count, metabolic panel, coagulation studies, troponin) follows. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman with Ehlers-Danlos syndrome has palpitations and lightheadedness on standing that ease when she lies down. Within 8 minutes of standing her heart rate rises from 72 to 108 while her pressure stays steady. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Neurogenic orthostatic hypotension","Vasovagal reflex syncope","Postural orthostatic tachycardia syndrome","Non-neurogenic orthostatic hypotension"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A rise of more than 30 beats per minute within 10 minutes of standing, improving when lying down, in a young woman with Ehlers-Danlos syndrome fits the syndrome, sometimes expanded as positional. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Where does the pain of acute coronary syndrome most commonly refer?","choices":["Straight to the back","Right lower quadrant","Right scapular region","Jaw, tooth or arm"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Acute coronary syndrome pain refers most commonly to the jaw, tooth or arm, but it can go to the back, the scapular region, or the epigastrium and left upper quadrant. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 48-year-old woman with rheumatic mitral stenosis has an echocardiogram showing a markedly enlarged left atrium. Which complication is she at greatest risk of developing?","choices":["Concentric hypertrophy","Left ventricular dilation","Atrial fibrillation","Aortic root dilation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In mitral stenosis the left atrium enlarges under raised pressure, and left atrial enlargement predisposes to an abnormal rhythm such as atrial fibrillation, which is common in severe disease. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 35, 39"},{"q":"A 53-year-old woman with severe symptomatic tricuspid stenosis is scheduled for valve replacement. Which valve type is preferred?","choices":["Bioprosthetic valve","Annuloplasty ring","Mechanical valve","Balloon valvotomy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. For severe symptomatic tricuspid stenosis, bioprosthetic valve replacement is preferred; balloon valvotomy and repair are reserved for hepatic congestion or severe systemic venous congestion. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"A 78-year-old man was treated in the intensive care unit with dobutamine for acute heart failure. His daughter asks whether he can continue it at home to keep his heart strong. What is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Only if his blood pressure is high","Yes, as long-term home maintenance","Inotropes are for intensive care only","Yes, in place of his other medicines"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Inotropic therapy, including dobutamine, norepinephrine, epinephrine and milrinone, is for intensive care use only. Outpatient therapy is guideline-directed therapy for his ejection fraction group. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 32-year-old man arrives for his first adult screening visit, having eaten lunch an hour ago. Which lipid test can be done today without fasting?","choices":["Triglyceride with high-density cholesterol","Total and high-density cholesterol","Triglyceride and total cholesterol","Calculated low-density lipoprotein"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Total and high-density cholesterol need no fasting; their ratio is the most efficient and cost-effective way to predict coronary risk. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man reports fainting twice after standing. Before any test is ordered, which part of his evaluation is most important for identifying the source?","choices":["A detailed history","A tilt-table test","An echocardiogram","A chest radiograph"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. History is the most important portion of accurately diagnosing the source of orthostatic syncope, or of referring to the right specialty for confirmation. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Why can nitroglycerin cause shock in a right ventricular infarct?","choices":["It provokes coronary spasm","It triggers major bleeding","Lost preload drops output","It blocks the atrioventricular node"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The infarcted right ventricle is preload dependent; decreasing preload reduces the Frank-Starling effect, the ventricle fails into right-sided heart failure, output falls and shock follows. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman on a statin has reached her low-density lipoprotein goal, but her triglyceride remains 260 mg/dL after lifestyle changes. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Add a fibrate or niacin","Continue with no change","Stop the statin and watch","Begin apheresis sessions"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The primary aim is the low-density goal, and if triglyceride stays above 200 mg/dL once it is reached, additional triglyceride-lowering therapy (a fibrate or niacin) is indicated. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 116"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman presents with acute right hemiparesis and aphasia of two hours' duration. Her blood pressure is 196/104. How should her blood pressure be managed?","choices":["Leave the blood pressure entirely untreated","Reduce the mean arterial pressure by a quarter in an hour","Reduce the pressure to below 130/80 immediately","Follow the acute stroke protocol, not chronic targets"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Acute stroke is specifically excepted from the general emergency reduction approach, because driving the pressure to a chronic target can extend the ischemic injury. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman with hypertension has weak delayed femoral pulses and a leg pressure lower than her arm pressure. Which study establishes the diagnosis?","choices":["Renal duplex ultrasound","Echocardiography","A morning aldosterone to renin ratio","Polysomnography"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the referral that follows is to a vascular surgeon or a structural cardiologist. The bedside findings raise the question; the echocardiogram answers it. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man with low high-density lipoprotein has improved his lifestyle, and his clinician is weighing a drug chosen specifically to raise high-density lipoprotein. Which drug raises it most?","choices":["Colesevelam","Niacin","Pravastatin","Ezetimibe"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Niacin raises high-density lipoprotein 15% to 35%, better than other medications, compared with fibrates 10% to 30%, statins 5% to 22% and ezetimibe 8%. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman with pulmonary hypertension has pulmonic regurgitation. She has no symptoms of right heart failure. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Pulmonic balloon valvuloplasty","Transcatheter valve replacement","Treat the underlying condition","Lifelong warfarin therapy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Management of pulmonic regurgitation is to treat the underlying condition -- here the pulmonary hypertension, the high-pressure cause -- with referral when it is seen on echo or right heart failure develops. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 64, 61"},{"q":"For reflex hypotension, what is the treatment principle?","choices":["Reduce or remove the medication","Treat the autonomic disease","Start a vasopressor infusion","Reduce or remove the trigger"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The principle is to remove the offending agent or trigger: reflex causes are managed by reducing or removing the trigger. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which drug is the most commonly used vitamin K antagonist for a mechanical valve?","choices":["Warfarin","Heparin","Clopidogrel","Aspirin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Warfarin is the most commonly used vitamin K antagonist, the long-term anticoagulant for mechanical heart valves; if surgery requires heparin instead, warfarin is bridged until the ratio is therapeutic. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"A 22-year-old healthy woman has no symptoms. On a routine examination a mid-systolic click is heard at the apex with no murmur. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Mitral stenosis","Mitral regurgitation","Aortic stenosis","Mitral valve prolapse"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Mitral valve prolapse usually affects healthy women aged 15 to 30, is usually benign and asymptomatic, and gives a mid-systolic click at the apex as the leaflets flare into the left atrium. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man starting home monitoring asks how to do it. What should he be told?","choices":["One reading each morning for three days","Readings only when he feels unwell","Two readings morning and evening for seven days","Continuous monitoring over twenty-four hours at home"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the accompanying instruction matters as much: do not overreact to the highest single reading, because acting on outliers generates anxiety and unnecessary changes in therapy. They are taken at least a minute apart and the average is reported. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 73-year-old woman with chronic heart failure has a B-type natriuretic peptide that has risen by 100 since her last visit. What does this change imply?","choices":["No change in her relative risk of death","About 35% higher relative risk of death","About 5% higher relative risk of death","About 90% higher relative risk of death"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Each increase of 100 carries about a 35% increase in the relative risk of death, so a rising level is a warning sign even when symptoms seem stable. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which finding is a sign of low perfusion rather than congestion in heart failure?","choices":["Breathless lying flat","Narrow pulse pressure","An enlarged liver","Distended neck veins"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Low perfusion shows as a narrow pulse pressure, sleepiness or obtundation, low serum sodium, cool extremities, hypotension on an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor and renal dysfunction. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man has crushing chest pain. His electrocardiogram shows ST elevation in leads II, III and aVF. Which artery is most likely occluded?","choices":["Left marginal branch","Diagonal branch artery","Left anterior descending","Right coronary artery"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Leads II, III and aVF are the inferior leads, and inferior ST elevation points to the right coronary artery (and/or the circumflex). The right coronary artery also supplies the atrioventricular node. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 61, 62"},{"q":"A 12-year-old boy has a harsh mid-systolic ejection murmur at the left upper sternal border that increases with inspiration. Which is the most appropriate test to evaluate the valve?","choices":["Chest radiograph","Electrocardiogram only","Cardiac catheterization","Echocardiogram"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pulmonic stenosis is diagnosed by echocardiogram, which shows abnormal valve motion and an increased jet velocity across the valve on Doppler. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man reads 124/78 in clinic, but his home average over seven days is 142/90. Why does this matter?","choices":["It carries real risk despite normal office readings","It indicates his home device is miscalibrated","It is a benign pattern requiring no follow-up","It means he has white coat hypertension"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. This is masked hypertension, and it is the reason out-of-office measurement is not merely a way of avoiding overtreatment: it also finds the patients whose risk the clinic reading conceals. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman reports six months of breathlessness when she walks her dog and one episode of fainting while gardening. She has never had cardiac imaging. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Electrocardiogram alone","Transesophageal echocardiogram","Transthoracic echocardiogram","Left heart catheterization"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Any patient with symptoms suggestive of aortic stenosis (exertional chest pressure, shortness of breath or syncope) should get an echocardiogram. Transthoracic imaging shows thickened, calcified leaflets with reduced opening. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 18, 22"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man with a sedentary job has a triglyceride of 280 mg/dL, low-density lipoprotein of 165 mg/dL and non-high-density cholesterol of 205 mg/dL. He has no xanthomas. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Severe hypertriglyceridemia","Mixed dyslipidemia","Familial chylomicronemia syndrome","Homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Triglyceride above 150 with low-density lipoprotein above 130 or non-high-density above 160 defines mixed dyslipidemia, the most common type in clinical practice. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man is ready for discharge after an ST-elevation myocardial infarction. His heart rate is 78/min with no bradycardia. Which drug class improves survival rates across the board after infarction?","choices":["Glycoprotein 2b/3a inhibitor","Calcium channel blocker","Beta blocker","Sublingual nitroglycerin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers improve survival rates across the board after myocardial infarction, so they belong in post-infarction management unless bradycardia or another contraindication is present. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A 40-year-old woman's mother had a myocardial infarction at 70 and her brother had one at 50. She asks which relative's heart attack raises her own coronary risk. Which answer is accurate?","choices":["Neither relative's","Only her brother's","Both relatives'","Only her mother's"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Family history counts when a male first-degree relative had sudden death or myocardial infarction before 55, or a female first-degree relative before 65. Her brother at 50 qualifies; her mother at 70 does not. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man has breathlessness and orthopnea (breathlessness lying flat). His chest radiograph shows blunted costophrenic angles, cephalization (prominent upper-lobe vessels) and an enlarged cardiac silhouette. Which study best measures his ejection fraction?","choices":["A repeat chest radiograph","Multigated acquisition scan","Right heart catheterization","Twelve-lead electrocardiogram"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The radiograph supports heart failure with effusion, cephalization and an increased cardiothoracic ratio, but cannot measure function. The multigated acquisition scan is the most accurate for ejection fraction. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 33"}]};
